{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"functions-basics","title":"Functions","group":"Functions","order":13,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A function is a named, typed block of code: a return type, a name, a parameter list and a body. It must be <em>declared</em> before it is called.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-code\"></i>Declaration vs definition</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nint add(int a, int b);                 // declaration (prototype): signature only\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; add(2, 3) &lt;&lt; '\\n';    // OK: the compiler has seen the declaration\n}\n\nint add(int a, int b) {                // definition: signature + body\n    return a + b;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Example</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Return type</td><td><code>int</code>, <code>void</code>, <code>auto</code></td><td><code>void</code> = returns nothing; <code>auto</code> = deduced from <code>return</code> (C++14)</td></tr><tr><td>Name</td><td><code>add</code></td><td>Can be overloaded (same name, different parameters)</td></tr><tr><td>Parameters</td><td><code>(int a, int b)</code></td><td>Each is a local variable initialised from the argument</td></tr><tr><td>Trailing return</td><td><code>auto add(int a, int b) -&gt; int</code></td><td>Useful when the type depends on parameters</td></tr><tr><td>Specifiers</td><td><code>inline</code>, <code>constexpr</code>, <code>static</code>, <code>noexcept</code>, <code>[[nodiscard]]</code></td><td>Change linkage, evaluation or guarantees</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-back-up\"></i>Return values</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n\nauto average(double a, double b) { return (a + b) / 2; }          // deduced: double\n\nstd::pair&lt;int, int&gt; divmod(int a, int b) { return {a / b, a % b}; } // two results\n\n[[nodiscard]] bool save(const std::string&amp; path) { return !path.empty(); }\n\nvoid greet(const std::string&amp; who) {\n    if (who.empty()) return;              // early return from a void function\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"Hello, \" &lt;&lt; who &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; average(3, 4) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    auto [q, r] = divmod(17, 5);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; q &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; r &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    greet(\"Ada\");\n    greet(\"\");\n    if (!save(\"out.txt\")) std::cout &lt;&lt; \"failed\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3.5\n3 2\nHello, Ada</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>[[nodiscard]]</code> makes ignoring the return value a warning — great for error codes and functions like <code>empty()</code> that are easily confused with <code>clear()</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>Function call mechanics</h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Arguments evaluated</div><div class=\"step-desc\">In unspecified order, then copied/bound into the parameters.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Stack frame pushed</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Parameters and locals live in the new frame (automatic storage).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Body runs</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Until <code>return</code> or the closing brace.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Frame popped</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Locals are destroyed (destructors run, in reverse order). The return value is handed back — often constructed directly in the caller's space (<em>copy elision</em>).</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Never return a reference or pointer to a local variable — the frame is gone after the return, so it dangles. Return by value instead; copy elision and move semantics make it cheap.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint&amp; broken() {\n    int local = 42;\n    return local;          // warning (error with -Werror): reference to stack memory returned\n}\nint main() { std::cout &lt;&lt; broken() &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i><code>main</code> and static locals</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint nextId() {\n    static int counter = 0;   // initialised once, lives for the whole program\n    return ++counter;\n}\nint main() {\n    nextId(); nextId();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; nextId() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is copy elision / RVO?</summary><div><p>When a function returns a local object by value, the compiler constructs it directly in the caller's storage instead of creating it locally and copying. Since C++17 this is <em>guaranteed</em> for returning a prvalue (<code>return Widget{...};</code>) and very common for named locals (NRVO). That's why returning big objects by value is idiomatic.</p></div></details></section>"}